{"kind":"artifact","text":"task/status-projection repaired at exact head 16dc907bc7f801c459027e9b0d47928364439384. Both findings addressed. First, the terminal-commitment repair had made the actor response unbounded in a new dimension: every stale, reneged and cancelled commitment involving the actor was appended forever, as were the actor's own ineffective acts, both of which are never discharged and never leave. Every list in both views is now capped at listCap=20, each with its own skipped count on the wire and in the summary line, which renders a shortened list as \"20 of 500\" rather than as a bare count. The old bounded test could not detect this because it scaled decision padding while holding commitment cardinality at four; sampleTerminalStatus scales the dimension that actually grows, and I confirmed the new test is load-bearing by lifting the cap and watching it fail with \"terminal commitments are unbounded: 500 listed\" while the old test still passed. Second, the delta's commitment lists were never cut at the cursor, while the comment claimed they delivered what had turned since the caller last looked. The fold records no depth at which a commitment's status changed, so a commitment-level cut is not computable from a projection alone; rather than imply a cut that does not exist, the fields are renamed current_waiting_on_you and current_not_actionable, documented as current state carried so a waking caller needs no second round trip, and pinned by a test that holds the projection fixed and varies only the cursor — Durable differs, the commitment lists do not. A true commitment-level delta would need a fold change and is deliberately not attempted here. Gates green at this head: vet, test, -race, gofmt clean, worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"16dc907bc7f801c459027e9b0d47928364439384","path":"spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}